# {{.Name}}

Golar {{.Template}} starter. Go 1.27 or newer is required.

```sh
go mod tidy
go test ./...
golar serve
```

Without the CLI: `go run ./cmd/server`. The backend defaults to :8080.
`golar routes` lists actual registered routes; `golar doctor` checks project tools
and files. `golar serve --addr :9000` overrides the address. Stop with Ctrl+C.

Folders: app/handlers, app/models, routes, internal/bootstrap, internal/config,
database/migrations, storage and tests. Add handlers in app/handlers and register
them in routes/routes.go. This starter has /, /health and /api/hello{{if ne .Auth "none"}}, plus the
account routes under /api/auth{{end}}.

Configuration uses .env, with existing process variables taking precedence.
Do not commit .env.
golar.yaml is a flat key: value manifest; strings may be quoted. Nested YAML,
anchors and multiline values are not supported. It stores scaffolding choices,
not secrets. JSON manifests are also accepted.
{{if .FrameworkDir}}
This project uses a local Golar replacement: {{.FrameworkDir}}. For distribution,
remove the replace directive and require a published Golar version.
{{end}}
{{if ne .Database "none"}}
## Database

{{.Database}} uses database/sql with the {{.Driver}} driver. DATABASE_DSN configures
the connection. Startup pings the database; shutdown closes it. Migrations live in
database/migrations; create one with `golar make:migration create_posts_table` and
apply them with `golar migrate`. Models in app/models use the Golar ORM.
{{if ne .Database "sqlite"}}Start the database and set DATABASE_DSN before serving.{{end}}
{{end}}
{{if ne .Auth "none"}}
## Authentication

Run `golar migrate` first: it creates the users table plus the API token and
password reset tables. Passwords are hashed with Argon2id.

| Route | Description |
| --- | --- |
{{if eq .Auth "session"}}| `GET /api/auth/csrf` | CSRF token for the current session |
{{end}}| `POST /api/auth/register` | Create an account (`name`, `email`, `password`) |
| `POST /api/auth/login` | Log in with `email` and `password` |
| `POST /api/auth/logout` | Log out (requires authentication) |
| `GET /api/auth/me` | The current user (requires authentication) |

{{if eq .Auth "token"}}Register and login return a personal access token. Send it as
`Authorization: Bearer <token>`; tokens expire after 30 days and only their SHA-256
hash is stored. Logout revokes the token that made the request.
{{else}}Login stores the user in a server-side session cookie. Every POST, PUT,
PATCH and DELETE request must send the token from `/api/auth/csrf` in the
`X-CSRF-Token` header; logging in rotates it. Sessions are kept in memory, so
they end when the server restarts. `.env` sets `SESSION_SECURE_COOKIE=false` for
local HTTP; remove it in production so the cookie requires HTTPS.
{{end}}
Register and login are rate limited per client IP, and failed logins per email.
Protect your own routes with `a.Required(handler)` in routes/routes.go. See the
Golar authentication guide for gates, roles, password resets and email verification.
{{end}}
{{if ne .Frontend "none"}}
## Frontend

{{.Frontend}} uses Vite. Requires Node 20.19+ or 22.12+ (Node 24 recommended).

```sh
cd frontend
npm install
npm run dev
```

Vite proxies /api and /health to http://localhost:8080. Set GOLAR_BACKEND_URL in
the frontend process to change the proxy target.
`npm run build` creates frontend/dist; the Go backend serves it in production.
Frontend HTML and assets are served by middleware and do not appear in the route list.
{{end}}
{{if .Docker}}
## Docker

```sh
go mod tidy
go mod vendor
docker compose up --build
```

Vendoring supports both published and local framework dependencies. Commit vendor
if your build pipeline needs to build without Go module downloads.
Compose database passwords are local development defaults; change them for deployment.
{{end}}
